"""Editorial revision; preserve equations, benchmark statistics and figure data."""
from pathlib import Path
import hashlib
import json
import re

HERE=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
path=HERE/'source_edited/DGF.tex'
before=path.read_text()
s=before
changes=[]

def block(start,end,new):
    global s
    a=s.index(start); b=s.index(end,a)
    changes.append({'kind':'section','start':start})
    s=s[:a]+new+s[b:]

def paragraph(prefix,new):
    global s
    lines=s.splitlines()
    found=[i for i,line in enumerate(lines) if line.startswith(prefix)]
    assert len(found)==1,(prefix,found)
    lines[found[0]]=new
    s='\n'.join(lines)+'\n'
    changes.append({'kind':'paragraph','start':prefix})

abstract=r"""\begin{abstract}
Data gaps can remove the early, high-amplitude cycles of a black hole ringdown. We present DenoiseGapFiller (DGF), an encoder--decoder that combines complex time--frequency features with a multiresolution temporal representation to reconstruct the clean waveform from noisy data. On a phenomenological benchmark of 1000 independent test signals with 33-s signal-suppression intervals, residual refinement and training-data augmentation reduce the mean direct noise-weighted mismatch from 0.02715 to 0.00537, an 80.2\% reduction relative to the DGF baseline. The mean absolute phase error at the target-envelope peak decreases from $4.32^\circ$ to $1.50^\circ$. These comparisons use fixed time and phase and include the full test population. We also formulate a two-channel LISA implementation with Kerr ringdown modes, a finite-arm response, second-generation time-delay interferometry and stationary instrument noise. This implementation combines PSD whitening, mask-aware refinement and a low-rank signal basis with a coefficient head conditioned on noise level and continuous phase. Its simulation and training protocol use 528-s windows and variable suppression intervals spanning approximately 6\%--10\% of the record. The benchmark results establish the reconstruction performance under the stated numerical signal and noise assumptions.
\end{abstract}
"""
block(r'\begin{abstract}',r'\maketitle',abstract)
s=re.sub(r'^\\noindent.*Revision status.*\n','',s,flags=re.M)

intro=r"""\section{Introduction}
The ringdown after a binary black hole merger is described, in the perturbative regime, by damped quasinormal modes whose frequencies and decay times depend on the remnant mass and spin~\cite{berti2006gravitational,berti2009qnm}. Measuring these modes enables black hole spectroscopy and tests of general relativity~\cite{Isi_2019}. Following the detection of GW150914~\cite{abbott2016observation,Abbott_2016}, space-based observatories such as LISA, Taiji and TianQin offer access to the ringdowns of massive black hole binaries~\cite{amaroseoane2017laserinterferometerspaceantenna,robson2019construction,Ruan2020,Luo2016}.

Ringdown amplitudes decay rapidly, so an interruption near the start of the signal can remove much of its measurable phase and amplitude information. Space-based observations are subject to scheduled interruptions and unscheduled data losses~\cite{Baghi2019,dey2021gaps}. Shi \textit{et al.}~\cite{shi2024effectsdatagapsringdown} studied their effects on ringdown observability and parameter estimation. A gap also redistributes power across Fourier frequencies~\cite{Harris1978}. Its impact depends on both duration and position: an interval covering 20\% of a short record can remove the merger and much of the early decay. The variable-duration LISA simulations considered here use intervals spanning approximately 6\%--10\% of a 528-s record.

Several methods treat incomplete gravitational-wave data. Interpolation estimates missing samples from neighboring observations, with accuracy set by the signal bandwidth, sampling and interpolation rule~\cite{Horowitz1974,Maeland1988,Unser1999}. Windowing reduces spectral leakage~\cite{Harris1978}. Bayesian data augmentation treats missing samples as latent variables and infers them jointly with source parameters~\cite{Baghi2019}. Sparse inpainting exploits Fourier or wavelet structure and has been applied to LISA galactic-binary signals~\cite{Blelly2021,Starck2010}.

Neural reconstruction learns waveform structure from simulated signals. Xu \textit{et al.}~\cite{Xu_2024} used a DenseNet--BiLSTM model to denoise inspiral--merger--ringdown signals under several forms of corruption. Mao \textit{et al.}~\cite{Mao:2025cae} combined a convolutional autoencoder with a bidirectional gated recurrent unit to recover long LISA sequences containing gaps. WaveFormer~\cite{Wang:2024waveformer} applies a Transformer architecture to noisy ground-based signals. Comparisons between these methods depend on the waveform family, detector response, noise and test protocol.

We develop DGF for joint denoising and reconstruction of ringdown waveforms. Its two branches retain different information: a complex time--frequency representation resolves local oscillations, while a multiresolution temporal representation preserves signed waveform context. The features are combined before sequence modeling and waveform synthesis. Mixing along temporal and feature axes is also used in related time-series architectures~\cite{TSMixer2023,TimeMixer2024}. For LISA $A/E$ channels, we add fixed PSD whitening, mask-aware and joint-channel residual modules, and a signal basis learned from clean training waveforms. A coefficient head uses absolute noise information and amplitude-weighted sine and cosine phase features.

We consider observations $d=Gh+n$, where $G$ suppresses the signal within one interval and instrument noise remains throughout the record. The network estimates the complete clean waveform from the observation and known mask. Paired comparisons use identical signals, masks and noise. The numerical benchmark tests the single-channel DGF baseline and its residual refinements; the LISA formulation specifies a separate physical source, response and noise model.

Section~\ref{sec:method} describes the network and LISA simulation and training protocol. Section~\ref{sec:results} presents the phenomenological benchmark results, and Sec.~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses their interpretation and subsequent inference applications. Appendix~\ref{app:model_analysis} gives implementation details, Appendix~\ref{app:sequence_modeling} defines the benchmark generator, and Appendix~\ref{app:complementary} reports additional diagnostics.

"""
block(r'\section{Introduction}',r'\section{Methodology}',intro)

paragraph('Both channels have fractional-frequency units.',r'Both channels are expressed in fractional-frequency units. We generate the response on a padded parent sequence, remove the padding and select a 528-s analysis window. Reconstruction is evaluated on that finite window, including for signals whose damping times exceed its duration.')
paragraph('A fixed calibration PSD',r'A fixed PSD $S_{c,\rm cal}(f_k)$ is estimated by averaging one-sided periodograms from 512 independent noise-only segments, each containing $N=1056$ samples at $f_s=2\,\mathrm{Hz}$. Estimating the PSD after cropping accounts for finite-window leakage near transfer-function nulls. The same calibration arrays define whitening, the training loss and the comparison metric for every model. The metric weights Fourier bins diagonally; likelihood calculations must also account for correlations introduced by the finite window.')
paragraph('Positive neighboring-bin values',r'At DC and Nyquist, positive neighboring-bin values define the whitening factors. Inverse whitening then restores the original fractional-frequency units. These two bins have zero weight in the primary overlap. The first 128 calibration segments determine the expected white-noise RMS, excluding DC and Nyquist, for the coefficient head. All calibration segments are independent of the signal population and test observations.')
paragraph('The primary gap widths',r'Gap widths are sampled uniformly from the 21 even integers $64,66,\ldots,104$, corresponding to 32--52 s or 6.06\%--9.85\% of the record. Changing the width preserves the reference interval center. Fixed widths of 66 and 100 samples provide additional duration checks. The parent response peak sets the event-centered simulation window, with reference gap offsets drawn between $-66$ and $66$ s. A second seeded draw places the reference interval start at local samples 132--263 and determines the crop. The network receives the resulting observation and known mask at inference.')
paragraph(r'Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} gives',r'Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} shows the DGF architecture. The two $A/E$ channels share encoder weights. The time--frequency branch represents a complex Morlet CWT by its modulus and argument. Sixteen scales and a factor-four temporal subsampling give a $2\times16\times264$ array per channel. Mixing along the temporal, feature and scale axes, followed by convolutional embedding and linear projection, produces $8\times32\times64$ features.')
paragraph('A physical-frequency adaptation path',r'A second Morlet CWT uses 16 logarithmically spaced frequencies from $0.002$ to $0.25\,\mathrm{Hz}$. Three convolutional stages with group normalization and GELU map these low-frequency features to an additive correction before the time--frequency branch projection.')
paragraph('The temporal branch normalizes',r'The temporal branch subtracts the observed mean, divides by the observed standard deviation and computes a db4 decomposition. Eight reconstructed subbands preserve signed time-domain information. Each is divided into 32 overlapping patches of 64 samples with stride 32, giving the same $8\times32\times64$ feature shape. The relation $1056=64+31\times32$ fixes the input length.')
paragraph('Elementwise addition fuses',r'The branch features are added and passed through token, convolutional and sinusoidal position embeddings. A residual Conv2d layer precedes 16 sequence blocks, each containing channel-wise scaled dot-product attention, normalization and a feedforward layer. The decoder maps features to waveform patches, averages overlapping samples, sums the subbands and applies a Conv1d layer. Restoring the observation normalization gives the channel prediction. Appendix~\ref{app:model_analysis} specifies the attention operation and checkpoint conventions.')
paragraph('A joint signal basis',r'We construct a joint basis $B\in\mathbb R^{2N\times128}$ from the 60,000 clean training waveforms in whitened coordinates. After removing DC and Nyquist, each waveform is normalized to give equal weight to each source. The leading eigenvectors of the resulting second-moment matrix form the basis. The basis and coefficient adapter produce the whitened prediction $b$.')
paragraph('Here $P_0$ removes',r'The operator $P_0$ removes DC and Nyquist in each channel. The scale $s=\operatorname{RMS}(u)\sqrt{2N}$ uses a small RMS floor for numerical stability. Equation~\eqref{eq:refinement} preserves the endpoint components of $b$. With a zero-initialized final layer, the head reproduces the starting coefficient prediction to floating-point precision.')
paragraph('All coefficient-head inputs',r'At inference, the coefficient head uses only the observation, current prediction, known mask and fixed noise calibration. Source parameters and clean targets enter simulation, training supervision and evaluation.')
paragraph("Each comparison uses the observation's",r'We evaluate the overlap at the original time and phase. For nonzero waveforms, $\mathcal M_{AE}\in[0,2]$, with values above one for anticorrelated predictions. An overall positive rescaling of the PSD cancels in the normalized overlap; its frequency dependence sets the relative bin weights.')
paragraph('These terms supervise both',r'The objective combines waveform agreement, a noise-weighted amplitude residual and a time-domain residual with three times the weight inside the gap. Losses and population mismatches are arithmetic means over sources.')
paragraph(r'Figure~\ref{fig:training_workflow} separates',r'Figure~\ref{fig:training_workflow} summarizes the training protocol. The training set contains 60,000 distinct physical sources. Each source contributes two variable-width observations and one fixed 33-s observation with an additional independent noise realization. Selected views undergo a global sign reversal of both observation and target. CWT and db4 features are recomputed for each view. One epoch contains 180,000 observations.')
paragraph('The validation population contains',r'The validation set contains 1,500 independent sources. Model selection uses the mean direct mismatch over this complete set with uniformly sampled variable gap widths. Fixed 33- and 50-s gaps provide additional checks on the same sources. The test protocol reserves 6,000 independent source seeds and generates their observations after model selection. Population summaries include all generated sources; SNR-conditioned summaries state the corresponding sample counts.')
paragraph('The saved DGF checkpoint',r'The DGF checkpoint, physical-frequency adapter and signal-basis adapter initialize the model. We compare three head configurations: continuous phase alone, absolute noise features alone, and both feature groups. Disabled feature groups are set to zero. Each pilot uses the same 6,000 training sources, a maximum of 60 epochs and patience 20 after a minimum of 20 epochs. The complete validation mean selects the configuration, with an exact tie resolved in favor of the combined head.')
paragraph('The selected head configuration',r'The selected head is trained on all 60,000 sources from a zero-initialized output layer. Caching backbone predictions and observation features allows the 264,128 head parameters to be optimized separately. AdamW uses batches of 1024, weight decay $3\times10^{-4}$ and a five-epoch warmup to learning rate $3\times10^{-4}$. The learning rate is halved on a validation plateau. Training has a maximum of 600 epochs, minimum 100, patience 100 and improvement threshold $10^{-6}$.')
paragraph('Selective fine-tuning updates',r'Fine-tuning updates the decoder, physical-frequency adapter, channel-wise and joint refiners, coefficient adapter and new head, while the encoder and transforms remain fixed. This stage has 422,151 trainable parameters. Group learning rates range from $5\times10^{-7}$ for the decoder to $10^{-4}$ for the new head. It uses batches of 128, the same weight decay, a maximum of 200 epochs, minimum 40 and patience 50. Validation plateaus trigger further learning-rate reductions.')
paragraph('Raw and exponential-moving-average',r'We compare raw weights with exponential-moving-average weights using decay factor 0.995 and the same validation mean. The initial checkpoint remains a candidate. At the end of each epoch, the checkpoint records model, EMA, optimizer, scheduler, shuffle and random-number states. Fixed-gap and SNR-conditioned scores accompany the primary selection metric. Numerical conventions are given in Appendix~\ref{app:model_analysis}.')

s=s.replace(r'\section{Retained legacy benchmark results}',r'\section{Numerical benchmark}')
paragraph('The numerical statistics and result plots',r'We test the single-channel DGF model and its residual refinements on the four-component phenomenological benchmark defined in Appendix~\ref{app:sequence_modeling}. The benchmark uses 528-s records with fixed 33-s signal-suppression intervals and the numerical signal and noise normalization specified there. The LISA $A/E$ construction in Sec.~\ref{sec:method} has a separate physical source, detector-response and noise convention.')
paragraph('In the earlier experiment we compare',r'We compare the DGF baseline, the first refinement and the augmented refinement on identical test observations and clean targets. The 1000 test seeds, 2026240000--2026240999, are disjoint from the refinement training and validation ranges. Every test signal contributes to the statistics. Linear interpolation replaces masked samples using neighboring noisy observations and leaves the rest of the observation unchanged; DGF estimates the clean waveform over the complete record.')
paragraph('The augmented refinement reduces',r'The augmented refinement reduces the mean $\mathcal M_S$ by 80.2\% relative to the DGF baseline and by 37.8\% relative to the first refinement. The paired mean differences are $-0.02178$ and $-0.003257$, with 95\% percentile bootstrap intervals $[-0.02697,-0.01696]$ and $[-0.00535,-0.00134]$. We use 5000 paired resamples and bootstrap seed 20261009. These intervals quantify test-sample variation for the fitted checkpoints; repeated training runs are needed to measure initialization variability. Figure~\ref{fig:mismatch} gives the full distributions, and Table~\ref{tab:results} reports their means, medians and 95th percentiles.')
paragraph('The clean-envelope peak lies',r'The clean-envelope peak falls inside the gap for 121 test signals. Their mean $\mathcal M_S$ is 0.14016 for the DGF baseline, 0.04580 for the first refinement and 0.02771 for the augmented refinement. The augmented median is 0.00320 and the 95th percentile is 0.11283. Its paired mean improvement over the first refinement is $-0.01809$, with 95\% bootstrap interval $[-0.03427,-0.00172]$. For the 879 signals whose peaks lie outside the gap, the augmented mean is 0.00229. Gap location therefore has a substantial effect on reconstruction accuracy.')
paragraph(r'Figure~\ref{fig:examples} illustrates',r'Figure~\ref{fig:examples} shows the three lowest-mismatch reconstructions among the 121 signals with peaks inside the gap. Their direct PSD mismatches are $4.08\times10^{-4}$, $4.89\times10^{-4}$ and $5.40\times10^{-4}$. The gaps contain 28.6\%, 30.2\% and 29.9\% of the respective target squared norms. The recovered waveforms closely follow the missing oscillations. These panels illustrate successful reconstruction; Table~\ref{tab:results} and the distributions use all 1000 signals. Additional cases are shown in Appendix~\ref{app:complementary}.')
paragraph('Normalized overlap is insensitive',r'Normalized overlap is invariant under a positive amplitude rescaling, so we also evaluate $E=\|\widehat h-h\|_2/\|h\|_2$. The augmented mean is 0.06496, compared with 0.13370 for the DGF baseline. Its median is 0.04753 and its 95th percentile is 0.14741. The mean signed norm bias, $\|\widehat h\|_2/\|h\|_2-1$, is $-0.00200$.')
paragraph('Inside the 121 peak-containing masks',r'For the 121 signals with peaks inside the gap, the local error $\|m(\widehat h-h)\|_2/\|mh\|_2$ has augmented mean 0.12349 and median 0.05735. The baseline mean is 0.33401. We also retain gap errors normalized by the complete target norm. Appendix~\ref{app:complementary} discusses the sensitivity of local normalization when the gap contains little signal.')
paragraph(r'Figure~\ref{fig:psd} compares',r'Figure~\ref{fig:psd} compares the mean periodograms of the reconstruction residuals $\widehat h-h$ and simulated noise. The augmented residual has lower spectral power than the baseline over much of the frequency range. These spectra describe the summed waveform; fidelity of individual components requires a component-resolved comparison.')
s=s.replace(r'\subsection{Phase recovery and alignment}',r'\subsection{Phase recovery}')
paragraph('The phase diagnostic uses',r'We evaluate the composite analytic phase at the target-envelope peak using the original time and phase convention. The augmented mean absolute deviation is $1.50^\circ$, compared with $4.32^\circ$ for the baseline. Its median is $0.73^\circ$, 90th percentile $2.69^\circ$ and 95th percentile $4.08^\circ$. Figure~\ref{fig:phase} shows the full distribution and the waveform from Fig.~\ref{fig:examples}(b), whose peak-phase error is $0.096^\circ$. Appendix~\ref{app:complementary} gives the peak-masking subgroup statistics and the dependence on the numerical waveform-to-noise norm ratio.')
paragraph('A separate matched-budget experiment',r'A matched-budget experiment tests refinement with a time-domain objective, with the mask, with spectral terms, and with both. Each variant uses 2048 training waveforms with two masks per waveform, 1500 validation signals, three initialization seeds and at most 100 epochs with patience 20. The mean composite validation scores are 0.03004, 0.02657, 0.02719 and 0.02440. Combining the mask and spectral terms gives the lowest score in each seed. This experiment isolates their contribution within the refinement module; the full backbone and long-run training variability require separate ablations.')

discussion=r"""\section{Discussion and Conclusions}\label{sec:discussion}
DGF combines a complex time--frequency representation with signed multiresolution waveform context. On the numerical benchmark, residual refinement and training-data augmentation reduce the mean direct noise-weighted mismatch by 80.2\% relative to the baseline and improve the mean peak-phase error from $4.32^\circ$ to $1.50^\circ$. Successful peak-masking cases recover intervals containing approximately 29\%--30\% of the signal squared norm. The complete test distributions also show that errors increase when the gap removes the dominant early cycles.

These measurements apply to the phenomenological generator and noise normalization in Appendix~\ref{app:sequence_modeling}. The LISA formulation introduces source- and detector-frame mass conventions, Kerr-mode excitation, a finite-arm TDI response and fractional-frequency instrument noise. PSD whitening, joint-channel refinement and noise-conditioned basis coefficients provide the corresponding reconstruction model. Its protocol covers single 6\%--10\% suppression intervals in 528-s records for nonspinning progenitors and an idealized equal-arm constellation. Quantitative assessment under these physical assumptions uses the independent $A/E$ test protocol of Sec.~\ref{sec:method}.

The reconstructed waveform is a point estimate conditioned on the observation. Extending the model to a moving unequal-arm constellation, nonstationary noise, confusion foregrounds, overlapping sources or multiple gaps requires simulations of those conditions. An uncertainty model and repeated training runs would characterize reconstruction uncertainty and variation between fitted networks. Tests of individual-mode frequencies and damping times would establish the accuracy relevant to black hole spectroscopy.

For parameter inference, the likelihood is constructed from the original residual $d-Gh(\theta)$ and the noise covariance. Reconstructed samples inherit correlations from the observation. They may serve as initial waveforms or sampling proposals, with posterior bias and coverage tested against that likelihood. Comparisons with other reconstruction methods~\cite{Baghi2019,Blelly2021,Xu_2024,Mao:2025cae,Wang:2024waveformer} should use common signals, responses, noise realizations, masks and overlap conventions.
"""
block(r'\section{Discussion and Conclusions}',r'\begin{acknowledgments}',discussion)

s=s.replace(r'\subsection{Representations and active sequence operation}',r'\subsection{Representations and attention}')
paragraph('For one $A/E$ channel, the legacy',r'For each $A/E$ channel, the backbone CWT array has shape $2\times16\times264$, and the temporal patches and fused features have shape $8\times32\times64$. For a feature matrix $Z\in\mathbb R^{32\times64}$, attention is computed as')
paragraph('Each of the eight feature channels uses',r'Each of the eight feature channels applies this operation, followed by feedforward transformations, residual connections and normalization. The implementation also allocates multihead-attention parameters that are inactive in the forward pass. The quoted total parameter count includes those stored tensors. Attention spans the full sequence.')
paragraph("The db4 implementation selects",r'The db4 representation uses the approximation and seven detail components in library coefficient order. The decoder combines reconstructed patches by overlap averaging and subband summation. Its linear patch map and the encoder embedding map have independent checkpoint values. When loading, we clone the initially transposed storage before reading the tensors so that their saved values are preserved on both CPU and GPU.')
paragraph('The registered final physical test',r'The $A/E$ test seeds are 2038200000--2038205999. The additional fixed-gap training-noise seeds are 2158200000--2158259999. Features are recomputed from each generated observation. The reproducibility record contains raw and EMA checkpoints, training histories, noise calibration, masks, seeds and per-source predictions. SHA-256 manifests identify the source files and fixed assets. Restarting resumes the last completed epoch with its optimizer, scheduler and random-number states.')
paragraph('The source prior and injected response peak',r'The simulated response peak determines the event-centered crop and gap metadata. Inference uses the cropped observation, known mask and fixed preprocessing arrays. Clean targets are used in the losses and evaluation. The implementation is available at \url{https://github.com/ljq2088/RingdownGapFilling}; configurations and checkpoint identifiers are included with the evaluation record.')

s=s.replace(r'\section{Earlier benchmark and retained numerical results}',r'\section{Phenomenological benchmark}')
paragraph('This appendix identifies the data',r'This appendix defines the signal generator, noise and training procedure used for Sec.~\ref{sec:results}. The physical LISA $A/E$ model is specified in Sec.~\ref{sec:physical_signals}.')
s=s.replace(r'\subsection{Earlier signal prescription}',r'\subsection{Signal prescription}')
paragraph('The earlier generator contains',r'The benchmark contains four oscillatory components labelled $(2,2)$, $(2,1)$, $(3,3)$ and $(4,4)$. Independent coordinates $M\in[10^5,10^6]$, $q\in[0.5,0.8]$ and $z\in[4,6]$ are drawn uniformly, with fixed orientation and phase. The coordinate $M$ enters through $MT_\odot$, while $z$ sets the distance scale. These coordinates define the numerical waveform family; physical remnant masses and redshifted mode frequencies are treated separately in Sec.~\ref{sec:physical_signals}.')
paragraph('with $b_a=(1,1,3,4)$',r'Here $b_a=(1,1,3,4)$ in the component order above. Table~\ref{tab:frequency_coefficients} lists the coefficients. The standard Kerr fits have $b_a=1$ for every mode~\cite{berti2006gravitational}. The last two benchmark components therefore follow the frequencies defined by Eq.~\eqref{eq:benchmark_frequency}.')
paragraph('They are numerical coefficients of this benchmark.',r'These multipliers specify the numerical component amplitudes. The physical excitation model in Sec.~\ref{sec:physical_signals} uses the relative-amplitude fits of Ref.~\cite{Kamaretsos2012}. Fixed angular factors and a TianQin-inspired modulation are applied in frequency space before the discrete inverse transform, cyclic shift, crop and amplitude scaling described below.')
paragraph('The frequency-domain function',r'The frequency-domain function \texttt{sf} is sampled on \texttt{arange}$(10^{-5},1,10^{-5})$ Hz with $K=99999$ entries. Its response-modulated component sum is passed to \texttt{irfft} with $N=2(K-1)=199996$ and the library normalization $1/N$. This places the first entry at the DC index. The implementation uses the array values directly, with no additional continuous-Fourier sampling-rate factor. After a cyclic shift of $N/2$ samples, 1056 samples are cropped from $(N-1056)/2+s$, where $s$ is a uniform integer in $[0,527]$. The crop is cast to float32 and multiplied by 0.03. These operations define the benchmark amplitude normalization.')
paragraph('Fixed angles are',r'The fixed angles are $\iota=\pi/4$, $\gamma=\pi/3$, $\theta_d=1.17662$, $\psi_d=-0.873302$ and $\phi_d=-0.615727$ radians. The TianQin-inspired modulation frequency is approximately $1.99\times10^{-5}$ Hz. These angular and modulation choices define the benchmark response.')
s=s.replace(r'\subsection{Earlier noise and refinement stages}',r'\subsection{Noise reference and training}')
paragraph('The earlier reference spectrum was',r'The noise reference is computed from three supplied arrays through $n_A=(n_Z-n_X)/\sqrt2$, using Welch segments of length 1024 and sampling interval 15 s. The arrays contain numerical values without detector-generation or unit metadata. Frequencies outside the reference range are filled with $10^{-40}$ in numerical PSD units. The normalization above defines the benchmark noise. An independent set of 200 cropped noise realizations determines $S_{\rm cal}$ for Sec.~\ref{sec:results}.')
paragraph('The earlier refinement used',r'Refinement uses 12,000 training waveforms and 1,500 validation signals. The augmented stage gives each training waveform six noise/mask views. Its loss weights the mask-enhanced relative time error, relative complex spectral error and direct spectral mismatch by $1$, $1/2$ and $1/4$. Model selection uses a composite score with weight 0.75 for the full validation mean and 0.25 for the peak-masking mean. The augmented run selects epoch 1095 from a maximum of 1200. The test uses seeds 2026240000--2026240999. Figure~\ref{fig:loss} shows the validation history.')
paragraph('This appendix documents the earlier benchmark',r'This appendix gives additional diagnostics for the same 1000 test signals used in Sec.~\ref{sec:results}.')
paragraph(r'Figure~\ref{fig:complementary_examples} retains',r'Figure~\ref{fig:complementary_examples} shows the median, 90th-percentile and maximum augmented-mismatch cases. The maximum direct PSD mismatch is 1.01221 and the maximum absolute peak-phase error is $130.09^\circ$. For seed 2026240571, the 126--159-s gap contains the envelope peak and 92.85\% of the target squared norm. The augmented prediction has 46.24\% of the target norm. Its mismatch is 1.01221, compared with 0.48104 for the first refinement. This source contributes to the full-population statistics.')

# Shorter captions describe the figure, selection and conventions once.
paragraph('\\caption{Implemented DGF reconstruction architecture.',r'\caption{DGF architecture. (a) The two channels share weights in the time--frequency and temporal branches, whose features are fused before encoding and waveform synthesis. (b) Mask-aware and joint-channel refiners feed a signal-basis adapter and noise-conditioned coefficient head. $P_0$ removes DC and Nyquist; the endpoint components of the intermediate prediction are restored before inverse whitening. Solid arrows mark the main path and dashed arrows mark conditioning inputs. Feature dimensions are per $A/E$ channel, with the batch dimension omitted. The small waveform, transform and network drawings are schematic.}\label{fig:pipeline}\label{fig:architecture}')
paragraph('\\caption{Source-separated data and staged training workflow',r'\caption{LISA $A/E$ simulation and training. Training, validation and test sources are disjoint. Augmentation uses training sources only; the signal basis is fitted to clean training waveforms, and whitening is calibrated on independent noise. Head selection and staged optimization use the full validation mean. The test observations are generated after the model and protocol are fixed. Dashed arrows indicate validation feedback. Epoch counts are stopping limits, and the small waveform, spectrum and optimization drawings are schematic.}\label{fig:training_workflow}')
paragraph('\\caption{Direct reconstruction mismatch for all 1000',r'\caption{Direct reconstruction mismatch on all 1000 test signals, evaluated at fixed time and phase. Means assign equal weight to each signal. Interpolation retains the observed noise outside the mask.}\label{tab:results}')
paragraph('\\caption{Empirical distributions of direct time-domain',r'\caption{Empirical distributions of direct time-domain and noise-weighted mismatch for all 1000 test signals. Annotations compare the baseline and augmented means. The logarithmic axis resolves the central distribution and upper tail. Interpolation uses the noisy observations outside the mask.}\label{fig:mismatch}\end{figure*}')
paragraph('\\caption{High-fidelity reconstructions selected',r'\caption{The three lowest-mismatch reconstructions in the 121-signal subgroup with clean-envelope peaks inside the gap, ranked by augmented direct PSD mismatch. Shading marks the 33-s suppression interval. Each panel divides the target and both predictions by the same target peak amplitude for display; the reported mismatches use the original arrays at fixed time and phase. Population statistics include all 1000 signals.}\label{fig:examples}\end{figure*}')
paragraph('\\caption{Mean one-sided periodograms of the simulated noise',r'\caption{Mean one-sided periodograms of the simulated noise and baseline/augmented reconstruction residuals over all 1000 test signals. Units are numerical benchmark amplitude squared per hertz.}\label{fig:psd}\end{figure}')
paragraph('\\caption{(a) Complete absolute peak-phase distributions',r'\caption{(a) Absolute peak-phase error distributions for all 1000 signals, with a linear scale near zero and a logarithmic scale above $0.2^\circ$. (b) Composite analytic phase for seed 2026240471, also shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:examples}(b). Phases share an unwrapped branch that preserves their relative phase modulo $2\pi$. Curves are displayed where the target envelope exceeds 10\% of its peak. Shading marks the gap and the dotted line marks the envelope peak. Time and phase are fixed.}\label{fig:phase}\end{figure*}')
paragraph('\\caption{Retained validation history',r'\caption{Validation history for the numerical benchmark. The full-population mismatch and composite score use the weighting defined in this appendix.}\label{fig:loss}\end{figure}')
paragraph('\\caption{Complementary earlier-benchmark cases selected',r'\caption{Cases at ranks 500, 900 and 1000 in ascending augmented direct PSD mismatch over the 1000 test signals. The target and baseline/augmented predictions use the numerical benchmark amplitude units at fixed time and phase. Gray shading marks the 33-s suppression interval.}\label{fig:complementary_examples}\end{figure*}')
paragraph('\\caption{Complementary population diagnostics for every',r'\caption{Additional diagnostics for all 1000 test signals. Left: absolute composite phase errors at the target-envelope peak. Right: direct PSD mismatch versus the numerical norm ratio $R=\|h\|_2/\|n\|_2$. Every point contributes to the population statistics.}\label{fig:complementary_phase}\end{figure*}')
s=s.replace('Coefficient values retained by the benchmark.','Frequency and damping coefficients of the numerical benchmark.')
s=re.sub(r'\n{3,}','\n\n',s)

# Protect numerical tables, equations and all figure assets against editorial drift.
def equations(src):
    return re.findall(r'\\begin\{(equation|align)\}(.*?)\\end\{\1\}',src,re.S)
assert equations(s)==equations(before),'Equation content changed during prose editing.'
def tables(src):
    return re.findall(r'\\begin\{tabular\}.*?\\end\{tabular\}',src,re.S)
assert tables(s)==tables(before),'Numerical table changed during prose editing.'
assert re.findall(r'\\includegraphics.*?\{([^}]+\.pdf)\}',s)==re.findall(r'\\includegraphics.*?\{([^}]+\.pdf)\}',before)
for marker in ['Revision status','registered run','during the present revision','will replace these entries']:
    assert marker not in s
path.write_text(s)
